A||||12||MooCow||Can I go from a stick to an auto?||||5||
Z||000000||MooCow||07-14-2000||10:21 PM||||I found a Fiero with a 4 speed stick with 130,000 miles on it. Lady driven very very nice everything works perfect and she only wants $1700.  It is a stick so can I change it to an auto??  How difficult is it to do this swap if possible?<BR>        <BR>                                 Thanks Ken||||reg||5
Z||000001||GT Bastard||07-14-2000||10:41 PM||||Yes, but it won't be cheap if you want someone else to do the work.  I'm sure there's plenty of auto guys that would trade from your manual car in a heartbeat.  Is it because you can't drive stick?||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FieroGTRacingInc||07-14-2000||11:29 PM||||IF...  it's a GT,   I'll trade you... I have a 87GT Automatic...    Steve..||||reg||1
Z||000003||MooCow||07-15-2000||11:58 AM||||I know how to drive a stick but I hear that sticks are no good in a Fiero because the clutch tends to go out really soon and cost like $1,000 to fix.  I don't have that kind of money to keep fixing the clutch.  The car is an 86 SE 4 speed with 130,000 but it runs VERY good.  It was owned by a very elderly women and she kept it in tip top shape.<BR>                 <BR>                             Thanks Ken||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||07-15-2000||12:05 PM||||The clutch is no less durable in a Fiero than it is in any other manual car.  An automatic can crap-out too, and could easily cost more than 1,000 bucks.  If the money is really a factor, you might be better off buying a Honda or something.  Then again, I've heard plenty of horror stories about the almighty Civic.  Bottom line:  If you're going to buy an old, car you'd better expect to shell out some money in repairs.  Good luck... ||||reg||1
Z||000005||kong||07-15-2000||12:15 PM||||  Moocow:   I've had a 5 sp fiero for 4-5 years now. I bought it needing a clutch. I put a new master and slave cylinder, and a new clutch,pressure plate,T.O. $325 for everything. After I found the right way to bleed the system I haven't had a single problem with it. save your money and keep the 4 sp.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||07-15-2000||01:44 PM||||Just to answer the original question, Yes it can be done. No, it's not easy.<P>It requires a diferant PROM for the ECM.<P>You need to rewire the ECM to control the torque converter lock. You could do without the lock but gas millage will bite and the tranny will run hotter.<P>You may need to change the radiator or at the least add a tranny cooler unit. You'll need to run all the cooler lines from the back for it.<P>You have to change shifters, eliminate the clutch interlock, wire for the neutral start switch on the auto.||||reg||1
Z||000007||286SE'S||07-17-2000||03:38 PM||||It was also mentioned in a previous thread on this that the steering column must be changed as well for the anti-theft locking to work when the lever is placed in Park. That thread also mentioned that it is easier to go from auto to stick. FWIW my clutch has been trouble-free for 40,000 miles.<BR>Terry ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula||07-17-2000||03:57 PM||||286SE......... Do you have 286 SEs or do you have 2 86 SEs....... if you have 286 fieros then you are the man!!!!! and if you have 2 86 fieros then you only need 284 more!!!||||reg||1
Z||000009||286SE'S||07-18-2000||05:52 PM||||Actually my computer is as slow as an Intel 80286. Wouldn't mind having the 284 Fieros but only have room for two.<BR>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000010||Old Lar||07-18-2000||06:10 PM||||Time out.  What would the cost estimate be to convert a manual to an automatic??  The same potential to spend $1000 for the conversion would be the same cost to fix a manual transmission.  If the manual transmission doesn't need fixing you are ahead of the game. But (big but) a car with 130K miles on it could probably use an new clutch.  If you get one replaced at a good shop it should be a trouble free fix for another 130K miles.  Then there is the engine life.<P>I had my clutch replaced at 75000 mile at a good shop and the car runs great.  (Preventative maintenance)||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||07-18-2000||08:20 PM||||I bought my v-6 5-speed back when it had 140K miles. The owner told me it still had the original clutch in it(almost all highway miles), and he had had no troubles with the clutch system at all. There were no signs that the clutch was failing or was going to fail. I decided to go on the safe side and replace it anyway. I opened everything up, and that clutch still looked brand new. There was hardly any wear on the friction material. The components were loose(clutch disk springs had lost all tension) and rattled when manually shaken, but the clutch system probably could have gone another 50,000-100,000 miles with no problems. I replaced the clutch with a heavy duty RAM clutch set, and have put a little over 20,000 trouble free miles. I don't think the clutch system in a Fiero is any less reliable than the next car. Sure, bleeding the system might be a pain, but with good master and slave cylinders, and a good clutch set, nothing should prevent a manual Fiero from driving any worse than another model car.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||07-18-2000||11:48 PM||||Figure I should add:<P>Remember, the engine tranny setup is STRAIGHT out of a FWD car. Fieros are no different(other than tranny cable routing) from any other 2.8 or 2.5 manual GM car. Same tranny, same engine, same engine and tranny mounts. Basically the cradle was taken from the front of a FWD car, and installed in the rear, with very little modifictation to the engine/tranny/suspension setup. ||||reg||1
